{"reference":"Jeremiah 25","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":25,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Historical and Textual Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jeremiah 25 places its warning in Judah’s transition from Josiah’s time into Babylonian domination. The chapter’s seventy-year announcement is associated with Babylonian rule and later restoration, and later readers interpreted that period in different ways. There is also a textual question behind the chapter’s list of nations: the Masoretic form of Jeremiah and the Old Greek form preserve markedly different ancient editions, with the Greek form shorter and placing the nation-oracles earlier in a different order.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 25:1-14","Jeremiah 25:1-38","Jeremiah 25:11-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_025_30982d25f0300ca2d6145289"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Jeremiah in Its Larger Book","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Jeremiah 25 stands at the close of a large opening section of the book. The received book of Jeremiah brings together poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, restoration collections, nation-oracles, and an appendix; its present shape reflects a long compositional and textual history. This helps explain why the book should be read as a varied collection rather than as one uninterrupted speech or narrative.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_025_c8b8eeb59dce2318eb867376"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}